\begin{theorem}[$F_{20}$ row]
Let $F_{20}=\{x\mapsto ax+b\}$ on $\mathbb{Z}/5$, the Galois group of $x^5-2$; its derived subgroup is $C_5$ and its coset readout is $a$. For the coarse type, $H(c)=2$, $H(T)=\tfrac{11}{10}+\tfrac14\log_25$ and $H(c,T)=\tfrac85+\tfrac14\log_25$. Hence $\Ilaw(F_{20})=\tfrac32$, with loss $\log_2[G:G']-I=\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The type cells have sizes $1,4,5,10$: the identity, $5$-cycles, type $1\,2^2$ ($a=-1$) and type $1\,4$ ($a=\pm2$). The joint cells have sizes $1,4,5,5,5$. The only confusion is between $a=2$ and $a=3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Law collision]
$\Ilaw(F_{20})=\Ilaw(C_4)=\tfrac32$, although $|F_{20}|=5|C_4|$ and $F_{20}$ is nonabelian.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[$D_4$ generator closes to $S_4$]
The $4$-cycle $(0\,1\,2\,3)$ and $(0\,2)$ lie in $D_4$, while $(0\,1)\notin D_4$, and $\langle(0\,1\,2\,3),(0\,1)\rangle=S_4$. Moreover $\Ilaw(D_4)-\Ilaw(S_4)=\tfrac54-\tfrac38\log_23>\tfrac12$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[$F_{20}$ seeded as $C_5$]
$\Ilaw(C_5)=\log_25-\tfrac85<\tfrac34$, and $\Ilaw(F_{20})-\Ilaw(C_5)>\tfrac34$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Diagnosis of the S3d anomaly}

The field S3d is a cubic field with group $S_3$ read through a sparse dial of about $229$ residue classes. Its historical channel value was $1.0078$; the simultaneous re-measurement gave $0.9998\pm0.001$.

\begin{theorem}
Every uniform Artin dial over the sign readout of $S_3$ has population channel exactly $1$ bit. Hence $1.0078-\Ilaw>0.0075$ for every dial, while $|0.9998-\Ilaw|<0.003$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sparse-dial saturation]
If a dial $r$ is injective on a finite sample $S$, then $\hat I_S(r;T)=\hat H_S(T)$ for every $T$, whatever the law.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The readouts $r$ and $(r,T)$ induce the same (discrete) partition, so $H(r,T)=H(r)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $x^3-x-1$ and the mod-$23$ dial, the sample $\{2,5,59\}$ has root counts $0,1,3$ and lies in distinct classes. Its plug-in channel is $\log_23\approx1.585>1.0078>1=\Ilaw$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}[Quantitative bias]
A first-order expansion gives $\mathbb{E}\hat I_n-\Ilaw\approx(K_{rT}-K_r-K_T+1)/(2n\ln2)$, where the $K$'s count occupied cells. For an $S_3$ dial with $K$ classes this is about $(K/2-2)/(2n\ln2)$. Simulation of ideal Chebotarev samples agrees with the formula. With $K=228$ the mean bias is $+0.0082$ at $n=10^4$ and $+0.0008$ at $n=10^5$, which matches the size of the historical anomaly. At $n=295{,}946$ it is below $3\times10^{-4}$. The ground-truth dictionary check rules out dictionary drift. The diagnosis is small-population plug-in bias on a sparse dial.
\end{remark}

\section{The simultaneous measurement}

There were fifteen canonical fields, one protocol, one seed, and $295{,}946$ unramified primes below $2^{22}$ per field. Law values were computed fresh from explicit permutation groups. The pre-stated tolerance was $\max(0.01,3\sigma)$. \emph{Result:} the global maximum $|I_{\mathrm{meas}}-\Ilaw|$ was $4.8\times10^{-4}$ bits, $20\times$ inside the budget, and no field was flagged.

The controls were as follows. The thickening control gave $-0.00044$ bits. Coprime flatness on six fields was below the null bias floor. Ground-truth factorization gave $0$ mismatches on all fifteen fields. The abelian dictionaries were $100\%$ correct. The fresh constants matched the hand-derived values to six decimals.

Seven errors were caught, all before results were examined. They include the $D_4$ generator closing to $S_4$, $F_{20}$ seeded as $C_5$, an exponent bit-order mismatch, and an incorrect ramification test for $q^2\mid\mathrm{disc}$. The programme's record of about 128 measurements is internally consistent to $5\times10^{-4}$ bits.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Law value from generators:} close the generators by breadth-first search, close the commutators to get $G'$, label the cosets, and compute $H(c)+H(T)-H(c,T)$. The cost is $O(|G|^2)$.
\item \emph{Fibre-product verification:} build $\{(g,r):c(g)=\varphi(r)\}$, check uniformity, and compare $I(r;T)$ with $I(c;T)$. The cost is $O(|G||R|)$.
\item \emph{Dictionary on real primes:} sieve, count roots mod $p$, compare with the Artin map, and compute the plug-in channel on prefixes.
\item \emph{Bias simulation:} sample $n$ points from the fibre product, average $\hat I_n$, and compare with the first-order prediction.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion and future work}

The audit separates the \emph{law}, the \emph{protocol} and the \emph{estimator}. The theorems above show that the protocol (thickening, joint measurement, choice of dial) cannot affect the law. Every remaining discrepancy is therefore an estimator effect.

\begin{conjecture}[Miller--Madow law for type channels]
For a uniform $K$-class dial and $n$ Chebotarev-equidistributed primes, $\mathbb{E}\hat I_n=\Ilaw+B/n+O(n^{-2})$. Here $B>0$ is of order $Kt$ and is computable cell by cell from the support of the fibre product.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Abelianization-shadow collisions]
Two groups have the same type channel whenever the pushforwards of their (coset, type) laws agree. Which Frobenius groups $C_p\rtimes C_{p-1}$ collide with cyclic groups, as $F_{20}$ and $C_4$ do?
\end{conjecture}

Further directions include certified bias-corrected estimators, simultaneous measurement of fields that are not linearly disjoint, and certified constants for larger groups.

\section{Conclusion}

A full table of type-channel law values was measured simultaneously and reproduced to $4.8\times10^{-4}$ bits. Exact theorems guarantee that uniform populations reproduce every Frobenius law, that product populations carry zero cross-information, and that residue dials of any fineness carry exactly the abelianization-coset law. The single anomaly is sparse-dial plug-in bias, which in the extreme sparse regime saturates at the type entropy, as the primes $2,5,59$ show by reaching $\log_23$ bits.

